===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Concerned with [[beauty]], [[artistic]] effect, or [[appearance]].
#: {{cot|en|cosmetic}}
#: {{ux|en|It works well enough, but the shabby exterior offends his '''aesthetic''' sensibilities.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=w:W. S. Gilbert|title=[[w:Patience (opera)|Patience]]|section=act I
|passage=If you're anxious for to shine in the high '''aesthetic''' line as a man of culture rare,<br>You must get up all the germs of the transcendental terms, and plant them everywhere.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1941|month=August|author=C. Hamilton Ellis|title=The English Station|journal=Railway Magazine|page=358|text=If Euston is not typically English, St. Pancras is. Its façade is a nightmare of improbable Gothic. It is fairly plastered with the '''aesthetic''' ideals of 1868, and the only beautiful thing about it is Barlow's roof. It is haunted by the stuffier kind of ghost. Yet there is ''something'' about the ordered whole of St. Pancras that would make demolition a terrible pity.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011| title=Jerusalem: The Biography – A History of the Middle East|author=Simon Sebag Montefiore | page=268| passage=But he was also a natural chronicler: one senses that, even as his schemes collapsed, this '''aesthetic''' Arab Quixote knew the stories would make great material for his witty, sharp, melancholic writings.}}
# {{lb|en|rarely|proscribed}} [[beautiful|Beautiful]] or appealing to one's sense of [[beauty]] or [[art]].
#: {{syn|en|aesthetical|tasteful}}
#: {{ant|en|inaesthetic|unaesthetic}}
#: {{ux|en|The design of the lobby cannot be considered particularly '''aesthetic'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2022 January 12|author=Paul Bigland|title=Fab Four: the nation's finest stations: Wakefield Kirkgate|journal=RAIL|issue=948|page=28|text=The station was rebuilt yet again by British Rail in 1967, when large chunks of the 19th century station were demolished and replaced with 'modern' buildings that were less than '''aesthetic'''.}}

